Here's a Java code snippet that finds the shortest path between two nodes in a graph using Breadth-First Search (BFS) algorithm:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Solution {
    public int[] bfs(String[][] graph, String start, String end) {
        int rows = graph.length;
        int cols = graph[0].length;

        // Check if the start or end node is out of bounds
        for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++) {
                if (i == 0 || j == 0) continue;
                String rowStart = charToRow(i, start.charAt(0) - 'a');
                String colStart = charToCol(j, start.charAt(1) - 'a');

                // Check if the end node is out of bounds
                if (i == rows - 1 || j == cols - 1) continue;

                String rowEnd = charToRow(i + 1, end.charAt(0) - 'a');
                String colEnd = charToCol(j + 1, end.charAt(1) - 'a');

                // Check if there is a path between the start and end nodes
                if (graph[i][j].equals(rowStart) && graph[i][j].equals(colEnd)) {
                    return new int[] {i + 1, j + 1};
                }
            }
        }

        // If no path is found, return null
        return null;
    }

    private String charToRow(int col, String c) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 0; i < col; i++) {
            sb.append(c);
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    private String charToCol(String row, int c) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 0; i < row.length(); i++) {
            sb.append(c);
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution solution = new Solution();

        String[][] graph = {
                {"A", "B", "C"},
                {"D", "E", "F"},
                {"G", "H", "I"}
        };

        String start = "A";
        String end = "I";

        int[] result = solution.bfs(graph, start, end);

        if (result != null) {
            System.out.println("Shortest path from " + start + " to " + end + ": [" + result[0] + ", " + result[1] + "]");
        } else {
            System.out.println("No path found");
        }
    }
}
```

This code defines a `bfs` method that takes the graph as input and returns an array of two integers representing the row and column indices of the end node. The `main` method demonstrates how to use this method with a sample graph and print the result.

The algorithm works by using Breadth-First Search (BFS) to traverse the graph level by level, starting from each node. It checks if there is a path between each pair of nodes at each step before moving on to the next one. If it reaches the end node without finding any paths, it returns null; otherwise, it returns an array of two integers representing the row and column indices of the end node.

The `charToRow` and `charToCol` methods are helper functions that convert characters to their corresponding row and column indices in a string representation.
